4. On an arbitrary ray $S T$, choose points $A_{1}, A_{2}, \ldots, A_{n}$ such that their distances from the origin of the ray $S$ are equal to $\operatorname{tg} \alpha_{1}, \operatorname{tg} \alpha_{2}, \ldots, \operatorname{tg} \alpha_{n}$, respectively.

At points $A_{1}, A_{2}, \ldots, A_{n}$, place masses $\cos \alpha_{1}, \cos \alpha_{2}, \ldots, \cos \alpha_{n}$. Let $Z$ be the center of gravity of the resulting system of $n$ material points. From the definition of the center of gravity, it is clear that point $Z$ lies within the segment $A_{1} A_{n}$, i.e., $S A_{1}<S Z<S A_{n}$. However, by formula (2) from the solution of problem 2 (p. 137),

$$
\begin{aligned}
& S Z=\frac{\cos \alpha_{1} \cdot \operatorname{tg} \alpha_{1}+\cos \alpha_{2} \cdot \operatorname{tg} \alpha_{2}+\ldots+\cos \alpha_{n} \cdot \operatorname{tg} \alpha_{n}}{\cos \alpha_{1}+\cos \alpha_{2}+\ldots+\cos \alpha_{n}}= \\
& =\frac{\sin \alpha_{1}+\sin \alpha_{2}+\ldots+\sin \alpha_{n}}{\cos \alpha_{1}+\cos \alpha_{2}+\ldots+\cos \alpha_{n}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Moreover, $S A_{1}=\operatorname{tg} \alpha_{1}, S A_{n}=\operatorname{tg} \gamma_{n}$. Therefore,

