1. Indeed, let the condition of Ceva be satisfied. Then, in $B$, we choose an arbitrary mass $m_{2} \neq 0$, and in $A$ and $C$, masses $m_{1}$ and $m_{3}$ such that conditions (13) and (14) of § 3 are satisfied, i.e.,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{A C_{1}}{C_{1} B}=\frac{m_{2}}{m_{1}} \\
& \frac{B A_{1}}{A_{1} C}=\frac{m_{3}}{m_{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

This is obviously always possible. From (12) (the condition of Ceva), $\left(13^{\prime}\right)$, and ( $14^{\prime}$ ), it follows that

$$
\frac{C B_{1}}{B_{1} A}=\frac{m_{1}}{m_{3}}
$$

And this precisely means (by the "lever rule") that

$$
Z\left[\left(C, m_{3}\right),\left(A, m_{1}\right)\right] \equiv B_{1}
$$

Conversely, let it be possible to choose three masses such that conditions (13) - (15) or, equivalently, conditions $\left(13^{\prime}\right)$ - (15') are satisfied. Multiplying (13'), (14'), and (15') term by term, we obtain (12), which is what we needed to prove.

15. Place masses of 1 unit at the vertices (see § 1, problem 13); the center of gravity of the entire system is the midpoint $S$ of the segment $O T$, where $O$ is the center of the circumscribed circle, and $T$ is the intersection of the diagonals. By Lagrange's theorem,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& J_{T}=J_{S}+4 T S^{2} \\
& J_{O}=J_{S}+4 O S^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

But $T S=O S$, so $J_{T}=J_{O}$, i.e.,

$$
T A^{2}+T B^{2}+T C^{2}+T D^{2}=4 R^{2}
$$

But by the Pythagorean theorem, $T A^{2}+T B^{2}=A B^{2}, \quad T C^{2}+T D^{2}=C D^{2}$. Therefore, $A B^{2}+C D^{2}=(2 R)^{2}$.

