48. a) Let the line $B M$ intersect $A C$ at point $B^{\prime}$, and the line $C K$ intersect $A B$ at $C^{\prime}$. Draw a line through $M$ parallel to $A C$, and denote by $P$ and $Q$ the points of its intersection with $A B$ and $B C$, respectively. Clearly, $\frac{\left|A B^{\prime}\right|}{\left|B^{\prime} C\right|}=\frac{|P M|}{|M Q|}$. By drawing a line through $K$ parallel to $A B$ and denoting by $E$ and $F$ its points of intersection with $C A$ and $C B$, respectively, we will have: $\frac{\left|B C^{\prime}\right|}{\left|C^{\prime} A\right|}=\frac{|F K|}{|K E|}$. We will make a similar construction for point $L$. Replacing the ratios involved in $R$ (see problem II. 43), we note that for each segment in the numerator, there is an equal one in the denominator, for example: $|P M|=|K E|$.

b) Let the line $l$ intersect the segments $C_{0} A, C A_{0}$ and form an acute angle $\varphi$ with $O K$. The line $A_{1} L$ divides the segment $M K$ in the ratio (from point $M$) $\frac{S_{L M A_{1}}}{S_{L K A_{1}}}$. Similarly, we find the ratios in which the sides $K L$ and $L M$ of triangle $K L M$ are divided. We need to prove that the equality $R=1$ holds (see problem II. 43). Replacing the segment ratios with the ratios of the areas of the corresponding triangles, $R$ will contain $S_{L M A_{1}}$ in the numerator and $-S_{K M C_{1}}$ in the denominator. We will prove that $\frac{S_{L M A_{1}}}{S_{K M C_{1}}}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin A}$, where $A$ and $C$ are the angles of triangle $A B C$. Clearly, $\frac{S_{B_{0} O A_{0}}}{S_{B_{0} O C_{0}}}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin A}$. Moreover, $\angle A_{1} B_{0} A_{0}=\angle C_{0} B_{0} A_{0} + \angle A_{1} B_{0} C_{0}=90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle B}{2}+\varphi$ (this follows from the fact that the circle with diameter $A O$ passes through $B_{0}, C_{0}$, and $A_{1}$) and $\angle B_{0} A_{1} O=\angle B_{0} A O = \frac{\angle A}{2}$. Similarly, $\angle B_{0} C_{1} O=\frac{\angle C}{2}$ and $\angle C_{1} B_{0} C_{0}=\left(90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle B}{2}\right) + \angle C_{1} O L=\left(90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle B}{2}\right) + (180^{\circ}-\angle C-\angle B_{0} O C_{1})=90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle B}{2} + (180^{\circ}-\angle A-\angle C-\varphi)=90^{\circ} + \frac{\angle B}{2} - \varphi$, i.e., $\sin \angle A_{1} B_{0} A_{0}=\sin \angle C_{1} B_{0} C_{0}$. Thus, $\frac{S_{A_{1} B_{0} A_{0}}}{S_{C_{1} B_{0} C_{0}}}=\frac{\left|B_{0} A_{1}\right| \cdot \left|B_{0} A_{0}\right|}{\left|B_{0} C_{1}\right| \cdot \left|B_{0} C_{0}\right|}=\frac{\sin \frac{C}{2} \cdot \cos \frac{C}{2}}{\sin \frac{A}{2} \cdot \cos \frac{A}{2}}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin A}$. Let $r$ be the radius of the inscribed circle, and $|O L|=|O K|=|O M|=a$. We will have:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{S_{L M A_{1}}}{S_{K M C_{1}}}=\frac{S_{L O M}+S_{L O M A_{1}}}{S_{K O M}+S_{K O M C_{1}}}=\frac{\frac{a^{2}}{r^{2}} S_{A_{0} O B_{0}}+\frac{a}{r} S_{A_{0} O B_{0} A_{1}}}{\frac{a^{2}}{r^{2}} S_{C_{0} O B_{0}}+\frac{a}{r} S_{C_{0} O B_{0} C_{1}}}= \\
& \quad=\frac{\frac{a}{r} S_{A_{0} O B_{0}}+\left(S_{A_{0} B_{0} A_{1}}-S_{A_{0} O B_{0}}\right)}{\frac{a}{r} S_{C_{0} O B_{0}}+\left(S_{C_{0} B_{0} C_{1}}-S_{C_{0} O B_{0}}\right)}=\frac{\left(\frac{a}{r}-1\right) S_{A_{0} O B_{0}}+S_{A_{0} B_{0} A_{1}}}{\left(\frac{a}{r}-1\right) S_{C_{0} O B_{0}}+S_{C_{0} B_{0} C_{1}}}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin A}
\end{aligned}
$$

(The last equality follows from the fact that $\frac{S_{A_{0} O B_{0}}}{S_{C_{0} O B_{0}}}=\frac{S_{A_{0} B_{0} A_{1}}}{S_{C_{0} B_{0} C_{1}}}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin A}$.) Similarly, we will isolate two more pairs of quantities in the numerator and denominator of the representation of $R$, the ratios of which will be $\frac{\sin A}{\sin B}$ and $\frac{\sin B}{\sin C}$, respectively. Thus, $R=1$. It remains to prove that the number of intersection points of the lines $L A_{1}, K C_{1}$, and $M B_{1}$ with the segments $K M, M L$, and $L K$ is odd.

184. Let $M$ be the intersection point of $N D$ and $A B$, and $P$ be the intersection point of the tangents to the circle at points $A$ and $D$.

Since the lines $N C, A B$ and $P D$ are parallel, from the similarity of the corresponding triangles we get:

$$
\begin{gathered}
|A M|=|D P| \cdot \frac{|A N|}{|N P|} \\
\frac{|M B|}{|N C|}=\frac{|M D|}{|N D|}=\frac{|A P|}{|N P|},|M B|=|N C| \frac{|A P|}{|N P|}
\end{gathered}
$$

but $|D P|=|A P|,|N C|=|A N|$. Therefore, the right-hand sides of expressions (1) and (2) are equal, i.e., $|A M|=|M B|$.

209. Notations: $A B C$ - the given triangle, $M$ - a point at a distance $d$ from the center of the circumcircle of $A B C$, $A_{1}, B_{1}, C_{1}$ - the feet of the perpendiculars dropped from $M$ to $B C, C A$, $A B, A_{2}, B_{2}, C_{2}$ - the points of intersection of $A M, B M, C M$ with the circumcircle of $\triangle A B C$, $a, b, c$ - the sides of $\triangle A B C, a_{1}, b_{1}, c_{1}$, $a_{2}, b_{2}, c_{2}$ - the sides of triangles $A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}$ and $A_{2} B_{2} C_{2}$, $S$, $S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$ - the areas of these triangles, respectively. We have:

$$
a_{1}=|A M| \sin A=|A M| \frac{a}{2 R}
$$

Similarly, $b_{1}$ and $c_{1}$ are found. From the similarity of triangles $B_{2} M C_{2}$ and $B M C$, we get:

$$
\frac{a_{2}}{a}=\frac{\left|B_{2} M\right|}{|C M|}=\frac{\left|C_{2} M\right|}{|B M|}
$$

Similar relations hold for $\frac{b_{2}}{b}$ and $\frac{c_{2}}{c}$. Triangles $A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}$ and $A_{2} B_{2} C_{2}$ are similar (see problem II.207); moreover,

$$
\frac{S_{2}}{S}=\frac{a_{2} b_{2} c_{2}}{a b c}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { Considering all this, we have: } \\
& \begin{array}{l}
\left(\frac{S_{1}}{S}\right)^{3}=\frac{S_{1}^{3}}{S_{2}^{3}} \cdot \frac{S_{2}^{3}}{S^{3}}= \\
=\frac{a_{1}^{2} b_{1}^{2} c_{1}^{2}}{a_{2}^{2} b_{2}^{2} c_{2}^{2}} \cdot \frac{a_{2}^{3} b_{2}^{3} c_{2}^{3}}{a^{3} b^{3} c^{3}}=\left(\frac{1}{4 R^{2}}\right)^{3} \frac{|A M|^{2}|B M|^{2}|C M|^{2} a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}}{a^{3} b^{3} c^{3}} \cdot a_{2} b_{2} c_{2}= \\
\quad=\left(\frac{1}{4 R^{2}}\right)^{3}|A M|^{2}|B M|^{2}|C M|^{2} \cdot \frac{\left|B_{2} M\right|}{|C M|} \frac{\left|C_{2} M\right|}{|A M|} \frac{\left|A_{2} M\right|}{|B M|}= \\
=\left(\frac{1}{4 R^{2}}\left|R^{2}-d^{2}\right|\right)^{3}
\end{array}
\end{aligned}
$$

(In the second equality, the similarity of $\triangle A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}$ and $\triangle A_{2} B_{2} C_{2}$ and equation (3) were used, in the third - formulas (1), in the fourth - formulas (2)). Remark. When $d=R$, the area of the triangle formed by the feet of the perpendiculars is zero, i.e., these feet lie on a single line. This line is called the Simson line (see problem II.153).

284. Let the sides of $\triangle ABC$ be denoted as usual: $a, b, c$; let $|BD| = d$, $|AD| = b_1$, and $|AM| = x$. We will use the generalized Ptolemy's theorem (problem II.239): $x a + (d - b_1 + x) b = (b - x) c$, from which we get

$$
x = \frac{b (c + b_1 - d)}{a + b + c}
$$

Take a point $N$ on $AB$ such that $MN$ is parallel to $BD$. We have:

$$
\begin{gathered}
|MN| = \frac{x}{b_1} d, \quad |AN| = \frac{x}{b_1} c \\
S_{AMN} = \left(\frac{x}{b_1}\right)^2 S_{ABD} = \left(\frac{x}{b_1}\right)^2 \frac{b_1}{b} S_{ABC} = \frac{x^2}{b_1 b} S_{ABC}
\end{gathered}
$$

Let $r$ be the radius of the circle that touches $MN$ and the extensions of $AN$ and $AM$. Then from (1) and (2) it follows that

$$
r = \frac{2 S_{AMN}}{|AM| + |AN| - |MN|} = \frac{2 x^2 S_{ABC}}{b x (b_1 + c - d)} = \frac{2 S_{ABC}}{a + b + c}
$$

i.e., $r$ is equal to the radius of the circle inscribed in $\triangle ABC$, as required.

