141. From the solution of problems 139v), g) it follows that

$$
\begin{array}{r}
C_{2 m}^{1}(1-x)^{m-1}-C_{2 m}^{3}(1-x)^{m-2} x+C_{2 m}^{5}(1-x)^{m-3} x^{2}-\ldots= \\
\quad=A\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{2 m}\right)\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{2 \pi}{2 m}\right) \ldots\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{(m-1) \pi}{2 m}\right)
\end{array}
$$

and

$$
\begin{aligned}
& (1-x)^{m}-C_{2 m}^{2}(1-x)^{m-1} x+C_{2 m}^{4}(1-x)^{m-2} x^{2}-\ldots= \\
& =B\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{4 m}\right)\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{3 \pi}{4 m}\right) \cdots\left(x-\sin ^{2} \frac{(2 m-1) \pi}{4 m}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

By equating the coefficients of the highest powers of \( x \) on the left and right sides in these two equations, we get:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& A=(-1)^{m-1}\left\{C_{2 m}^{1}+C_{2 m}^{3}+C_{2 m}^{5}+\ldots\right\} \\
& B=(-1)^{m}\left\{1+C_{2 m}^{2}+C_{2 m}^{4}+\ldots\right\}
\end{aligned}
$$

from which (see problem 55 a), b))

$(-1)^{m-1} A+(-1)^{m} B=2^{2 m}, \quad(-1)^{m-1} A-(-1)^{m} B=0$,

i.e.,

$$
A=(-1)^{m-1} 2^{2 m-1}, \quad B=(-1)^{m} 2^{2 m-1}
$$

Now, by equating the constant terms on the left and right sides in the same two equations, we have:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2 m & =C_{2 m}^{1}=(-1)^{m-1} A \sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{2 m} \sin ^{2} \frac{2 \pi}{2 m} \ldots \sin ^{2} \frac{(m-1) \pi}{2 m} \\
1 & =(-1)^{m} B \sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{4 m} \sin ^{2} \frac{3 \pi}{4 m} \sin ^{2} \frac{5 \pi}{4 m} \ldots \sin ^{2} \frac{(2 m-1) \pi}{4 m}
\end{aligned}
$$

from which the required identities follow.

164. This problem is solved similarly to problem 162a). Let \(ABCD\) be a curvilinear trapezoid bounded by the curve \(y = \frac{1}{x^l}\), the x-axis, and the lines \(x = 1\) and \(x = n\). Consider a step figure composed of \(n-1\) rectangles with a base of 1, inscribed in this curvilinear trapezoid, and a step figure composed of \(n-1\) rectangles with a base of 1, circumscribed around it (see Fig. 155, a and b). The areas of these step figures are

\[
1 + \frac{1}{2^l} + \frac{1}{3^l} + \ldots + \frac{1}{(n-1)^2} \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{1}{2^l} + \frac{1}{3^l} + \ldots + \frac{1}{(n-1)^2} + \frac{1}{n^l}
\]

and the area of the curvilinear trapezoid \(ABCD\) is \(\frac{1 - \frac{1}{n^l}}{l-1}\) (see the solution to problem 148a)). From this, it follows that

\[
