Let $r$ now denote the length of the period of the sequence $\bar{t}_{1}, \bar{t}_{2}, \ldots, \ldots, \bar{t}_{n}, \ldots$ and let $t_{1}$ not be a root of the equation $x^{2}-x-1=0$. We have $\bar{t}_{r+1}=\bar{t}_{1}=\infty$ and from the formula $\bar{t}_{r+1}=1+\frac{1}{\bar{t}_{r}}$ (see problem 128) it follows that $\overline{t_{r}}=0$. According to the same problem 128, the elements $\bar{t}_{1}, \bar{t}_{2}, \ldots, \bar{t}_{r}$ are pairwise distinct and, in particular, the elements $\bar{t}_{1}, \bar{t}_{2}, \ldots, \bar{t}_{r-1}$ are different from $\bar{t}_{r}=0$. By 1), from the equality $t_{r}=0$ it follows that $t_{r+1}=t_{1}$. In this case, all elements $t_{2}, t_{3}, \ldots, t_{r}$ are different from $t_{1}$, for if $t_{1}=t_{s+1}(s<r)$, it would follow (by 2)) that $\overline{t_{s}}=0$, which actually does not occur. Thus, it is established that the length of the period of the sequence $t_{1}, t_{2}, \ldots, t_{n}, \ldots$, as well as the length of the period of the sequence $\bar{t}_{1}, \bar{t}_{2}, \ldots, \bar{t}_{n}, \ldots$, is equal to $r$.

