$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(\frac{1}{2\left(k+k_{2}\right)}-\frac{1}{2\left(k+k_{1}\right)}\right):\left(\frac{k_{2}}{2\left(k+k_{2}\right)}\right. & \left.-\frac{k_{1}}{2\left(k+k_{1}\right)}\right)= \\
& =\left(k_{1}-k_{2}\right):\left(k k_{2}-k k_{i}\right)=-\frac{1}{k}
\end{aligned}
$$

The angular coefficients of the lines $P_{2} P_{8}$ and $P_{3} P_{1}$ will be exactly the same. Therefore, the points $P_{1}, P_{2}, P_{3}$ lie on one line (its equation: $k y+x=1 / 2$ ).

By connecting the point $H$ of the intersection of the altitudes of the triangle with the points $P_{1}, P_{2}, P_{3}$ by lines, we obtain the following interesting consequence. Let $\alpha_{1}, \alpha_{2}, \alpha_{3}$ be the angles of the triangle, listed counterclockwise, $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}$ be the lines on which the opposite sides lie; through the point $H$ pass three lines $p_{1}, p_{2}, p_{3}$ such that the angles between $p_{2}$ and $p_{3}, p_{3}$ and $p_{1}, p_{1}$ and $p_{2}$ (measured counterclockwise) are equal to $\alpha_{1}, \alpha_{2}, \alpha_{3}$, respectively. Then the points of intersection of $p_{1}$ with $a_{1}, p_{2}$ with $a_{2}, p_{3}$ with $a_{3}$ lie on one line. We suggest the reader consider special cases of this theorem (many of them are beautiful and far from obvious geometric facts) and compare it with problem 207.

One more remark: in our problem, instead of the midpoints of the segments cut off on the sides of the triangle, we could have taken points that divide them in the same ratios. These points will also lie on one line.

