Solution 3. First, we will prove the required statement in the case when point \(A^{\prime}\) lies on edge \(A B\). It is clear that \(\angle B A^{\prime} C = \angle B A C + \angle A C A^{\prime}\) and \(\angle B A^{\prime} D = \angle B A D + \angle A D A^{\prime}\). Therefore, the required inequality can be transformed into \(\angle A C A^{\prime} + \angle C A^{\prime} D + \angle A D A^{\prime} > \angle C A D\). Considering that \(\angle C A^{\prime} D = 180^{\circ} - \angle A^{\prime} C D - \angle A^{\prime} D C\) and \(\angle C A D = 180^{\circ} - \angle A C D - \angle A D C\), we transition to the inequality \(\angle A C A^{\prime} + \angle A C D + \angle A D C + \angle A D A^{\prime} > \angle A^{\prime} C D + \angle A^{\prime} D C\). This inequality follows from the well-known inequalities for trihedral angles: \(\angle A C A^{\prime} + \angle A C D > \angle A^{\prime} C D\) and \(\angle A D C + \angle A D A^{\prime} > \angle A^{\prime} D C\).

Now, the required inequality is easily proven in the general case. For this, we first consider the point \(A_{1}\) where the plane \(A^{\prime} C D\) intersects edge \(A B\). Then we consider the point \(A_{2}\) where the line \(C A^{\prime}\) intersects segment \(A_{1} D\). Applying the proven inequality sequentially to points \(A_{1}\), \(A_{2}\), and \(A^{\prime}\), we obtain the required result.

