Solution 5. Let \(A B C D E F\) be a hexagon satisfying the condition of the problem. Quadrilateral \(F B C E\) is an isosceles trapezoid, so the line \(M M_{1}\), connecting the midpoints of its bases, is perpendicular to them and serves as the bisector of the angle between the diagonals \(B E\) and \(F C\). Similarly, the properties of lines \(N N_{1}\) and \(L L_{1}\), connecting the midpoints of opposite sides of the hexagon, can be proven. These three lines are the angle bisectors of the triangle formed by the diagonals, so they intersect at one point \(O\). Point \(O\) is equidistant from all vertices of the hexagon, as it lies on all the perpendicular bisectors of the sides of the hexagon. Therefore, a circle can be circumscribed around the hexagon with center \(O\).

