Solve 5. That the triangle \(A B C\) is contained within the triangle \(A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}\) is obvious. We will show that the points \(A_{2}, B_{2}, C_{2}\) are the points of intersection of the sides of the triangle \(A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}\) with the lines \(A_{0} A_{1}, B_{0} B_{1}, C_{0} C_{1}\). Place masses \(1+k^{3}, k, k^{2}\) at points \(A_{0}, B_{0}, C_{0}\). The center of mass of this system is the point of intersection of the segments \(A_{0} A_{1}\) and \(B_{1} C_{1}\). Indeed, \(C_{1}\) is the center of mass of the points \(A_{0}\) and \(B_{0}\) with masses 1 and \(k\), \(B_{1}\) is the center of mass of the points \(A_{0}\) and \(C_{0}\) with masses \(k^{3}\) and \(k^{2}\), and \(A_{1}\) is the center of mass of the points \(B_{0}\) and \(C_{0}\) with masses \(k\) and \(k^{2}\). Thus, if \(A^{\prime}\) is the point of intersection of the segments \(A_{0} A_{1}\) and \(B_{1} C_{1}\), then \(B_{1} A^{\prime}: A^{\prime} C_{1}=(1+k):(k^{2}+k^{3})=1: k^{2}\), so \(A^{\prime}=A_{2}\). The proof for points \(B_{2}\) and \(C_{2}\) is analogous.

The proven result means the following. For the triangle \(A_{1} B_{1} C_{1}\), we do the same as for the triangle \(A_{0} B_{0} C_{0}\), only replacing the coefficient \(k\) with \(1 / k^{2}\); the triangle \(A B C\) remains the same. The resulting triangle \(A_{2} B_{2} C_{2}\) again contains the triangle \(A B C\) and so on.

